ntrast, a missing required proof\nartifact inside an intact `/candidate` mount is a candidate defect and may\njustify `FAIL / NOT_LEGIT`.\n\n### 2. Program fidelity and candidate-versus-canonical checks\n\nRestate the natural-language contract using `/reference/prompt.py` and\n`/reference/canonical.py`. Inspect `solution.py` over the intended input domain.\nRegenerate `solution.mpy` from `solution.py` with the trusted translator and\nrequire byte identity with the submitted `solution.mpy`.\n\nCreate an independent differential script that imports the trusted canonical\nentry point and the generated entry point. Exercise documented examples, empty\nand boundary cases, every branch boundary, and representative generated inputs.\nPreserve the script, inputs, commands, exit status, and results in\n`/audit-output/evidence/`. A different algorithm is acceptable; a material result\ndivergence on the intended domain must be judged.\n\n### 3. clean proof reconstruction\n\nFrom the scratch copy, discard or ignore all candidate-built definitions and\ncaches. Build the concrete and proof definitions from source using the semantics\nselected by the rendered mode. Independently run every positive target-proof\nclaim. A claim closes only when `kprove` exits zero and prints `#Top`; preserve\nthe command and bounded output for each claim. A missing artifact or positive\nclaim that does not close is `FAIL / NOT_LEGIT` unless the evidence establishes\nan audit infrastructure failure.\n\nFor `GENERATED_SEMANTICS`, also rebuild the generated semantics and concretely\nexecute it on normal and boundary inputs. Compare those results with independent\nPython execution and preserve the evidence.\n\n### 4. Adequacy and real-program pinning\n\nRestate each entry claim's precondition and postcondition in plain language.\nVerify that the `<k>` cell executes the actual submitted `solution.mpy` program,\nthat every helper or loop claim matches real control flow, and that the returned\nvalue is constrained to the intended result rather than a free variable,\ntautology, or one-way implication where equivalence is required. Exhibit at\nleast one state satisfying every entry precondition. Substitute concrete\nsatisfying inputs into the claimed result and compare it with both Python\nimplementations.\n\nThe claim need not literally read or begin with the complete `solution.mpy`\nmodule. It pins the submitted program when trusted regeneration plus a\nmechanical constructor-level comparison shows that the executed claim term is\nthe same function binding and body, allowing only demonstrated semantically\ninert normalization such as omitted typing-only imports or syntax macros that\nexpand to that term. Fixed semantics must still execute every material operation\nand control effect. Treat lack of automatic source-to-proof regeneration as an\nartifact-maintenance observation, not a proof failure for the immutable\ncandidate. A body-sensitivity mutation must change the program term actually\nexecuted by the claim; changing only an external source file while leaving that\nterm unchanged does not test the theorem's dependence on its body.\n\n### 5. rule-by-rule static soundness review\n\nCreate an exhaustive rule inventory for `semantic.k`, generated helper K files,\nand `verification.k` as applicable. Enumerate every local syntax declaration,\nfunction, `total` or `functional` declaration, opaque symbol, priority rule,\nordinary semantic rule, and simplification rule. Map every syntactic construct\nused by `solution.mpy` to its declaration and rules. Check configuration and\ncells, evaluation order and control flow, state changes and allocation, calls\nand returns, guards, overlaps, priorities, totality, and simplifications.\n\nDecide for every inventoried rule whether it follows from the selected semantics\nlevel, the real program behavior, and ordinary mathematics. Every unsoundness\nclaim must include a concrete or symbolic false conclusion witness that the rule\ncan enable on the intended input domain. Without such a witness, do not label the\nrule unsound; state the narrower evidence gap instead. Reject rules that encode\nthe task's answer, replace a property-bearing computation with an unconstrained\noracle, bypass real execution, silently fabricate results for unmodeled used\nconstructs, or otherwise make a false claim provable. Distinguish an acceptable\nlow-level trust boundary from a smuggled correctness conclusion.\n\n### 6. Fresh non-vacuity test\n\nInspect any candidate `spec-vacuity.k` only as untrusted evidence. In scratch,\ncreate a fresh, meaningful false mutation that changes a result-constraining\nobligation and is demonstrably false for at least one satisfying input. Require\nthe mutation to build successfully and the proof to fail because of the expected\nunmet obligation. A parser error, missing import, timeout, unrelated crash, or\nmutation that was never reachable is not non-vacuity evidence. Preserve the\nmutation and its build/proof logs.\n\n### 7. proven versus assumed accounting\n\nState precisely what the successful reachability proof establishes. List every\ntrusted primitive, opaque symbol, empirical bridge, and informal argument.\nExplain why each boundary is acceptable, concerning, or illegitimate. Do not\naccept `PROOF.md`, generated traces, or differential testing as a substitute for\nthe K proof; differential evidence may support only the bridge it actually\ntests.\n\n## Decision boundary\n\n- `PASS / LEGIT`: the reconstructed proof is sound, result-constraining, and\n  pins the real generated program, with no material adequacy gap.\n- `CONCERNS / LEGIT`: the reconstructed proof is sound, pins the real generated\n  program, and covers the material source-contract domain, but has a documented\n  non-fatal evidence or trust-boundary limitation. Informal intent bridges, thin\n  differential coverage, and over-broad-but-sound-on-the-intended-domain rules\n  belong here only when they do not make a false conclusion provable.\n- `FAIL / NOT_LEGIT`: the proof is missing, fails clean reconstruction, is\n  vacuous or non-constraining, proves a substituted program, materially narrows\n  the source-contract domain, or relies on materially unsound semantics or proof\n  rules. Finitely many fixed sizes, examples, or bounded unrollings do not prove\n  an unrestricted HumanEval domain. For every claimed unsound rule, include the\n  required false conclusion witness.\n\nDo not turn a timeout, container failure, model failure, malformed mount, or\nother audit infrastructure uncertainty into a candidate verdict.\n\nStructure `REVIEW.md` around the seven stages, cite file locations and evidence\nartifacts, and explain the verdict. For a completed audit, end the file with\nexactly these two lines, with a valid pairing and nothing after them:\n\n```text\nVERDICT: <PASS|CONCERNS|FAIL>\nLEGITIMACY: <LEGIT|NOT_LEGIT>\n```\n","images":[],"local_images":[],"text_elements":[]}}
